ng.md-56-   existing durable metadata, `<output_dir>/agent` when present, and\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-57-   `<output_dir>/trace` when present. The final merge result is copied to\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-58-   `metadata/<group>/<artifact>/<version>/reachability-metadata.json`, and\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-59-   the gate returns `PASSED`. If tracing stalls, exhausts its budget, or\\n--\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-78-\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-79-The gate closes those gaps with runtime truth rather than more inference, using\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-80-two native-image flags:\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-81-\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-82-- `--exact-reachability-metadata` makes the native image treat any access that\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-83-  is **not** backed by supplied metadata as a hard miss instead of silently\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md:84:  registering it. With `-H:MissingRegistrationReportingMode=Exit`, the binary\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-85-  exits `ExitStatus.MISSING_METADATA` (172) and prints the exact missing entry\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-86-  the instant it hits an unregistered access.\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-87-- The same `runNativeTraceImage` execution that detects the miss also records\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-88-  it: the missing entry is written into that cycle's trace directory as a\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-89-  ground-truth metadata entry (§WF-native-trace-gradle-tasks). Feeding that\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-90-  directory back through `metadataConfigDirs` makes the next build aware of the\\n--\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-115-### WF-native-tracing-reporter-divergence: Reporter and tracer are separate components\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-116-\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-117-Under the tracing build flags (§WF-native-trace-gradle-tasks), two\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-118-*independent* GraalVM Native Image components cooperate on each\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-119-`runNativeTraceImage` cycle:\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-120-\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md:121:- the **missing-registration reporter** (`--exact-reachability-metadata`,\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md:122:  `-H:MissingRegistrationReportingMode=Exit`) detects an access that is not\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-123-  backed by supplied metadata, prints the exact missing entry, and exits the\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-124-  binary with `ExitStatus.MISSING_METADATA` (172);\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-125-- the **metadata tracer** (`-H:+MetadataTracingSupport`) writes the accesses the\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-126-  execution observes into the trace directory named by `-PtraceMetadataPath`.\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-127-\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-128-**Invariant.** Every entry the reporter prints as missing in a cycle must be\\n--\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-235-  `user-code-filter.json` when available. Maven groups are often not Java\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-236-  package roots (for example an `org.apache.tomcat.embed` artifact can execute\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-237-  `org.apache.catalina`, `org.apache.coyote`, and `org.apache.tomcat` code).\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-238-  Using the package filter keeps tracing able to select a condition class that\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-239-  is actually on the access stack before a missing metadata access occurs. If\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-240-  the filter cannot be read, the legacy `[group]` fallback is used.\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md:241:- **Inactive metadata conditions are too-late conditions.** When GraalVM reports\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md:242:  that metadata for an access exists but is inactive because runtime conditions\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-243-  were not satisfied, the repair must move or duplicate the matching metadata\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-244-  under a condition reached before the access. The condition should be inferred\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-245-  from the access stack, usually the library frame performing the reflective,\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-246-  resource, proxy, serialization, or JNI access. Reusing the unsatisfied\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-247-  condition is invalid because it preserves the same timing failure.\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-248-- **Native test run second.** After JVM-agent metadata is generated, the\\n--\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-270-  -PtraceMetadataConditionPackages=<packages>\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-271-  -PmetadataConfigDirs=<config_dirs>` where `config_dirs` is the staged\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-272-  agent dir, when present, followed by accepted trace dirs. The last\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-273-  property is omitted only when no staged agent metadata and no accepted\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-274-  trace dirs exist. The resulting binary is built with `-H:+MetadataTracingSupport`,\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-275-  `--exact-reachability-metadata`, and\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md:276:  `-H:MissingRegistrationReportingMode=Exit` (see the `runNativeTraceImage`\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-277-  contract, §WF-native-trace-gradle-tasks).\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-278-  The same execution writes a trace dir **and** returns an\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-279-  exact-metadata-aware exit code.\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-280-- **Exit-code routing**:\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-281-  - `0` → the metadata accumulated in `config_dirs` plus the code under\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-282-    test are sufficient. The gate runs `mergeNativeTraceMetadata` to\\n--\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-383-Build-flag behavior:\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-384-\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-385-- The task adds `-H:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-386-  -H:+MetadataTracingSupport -H:-UnlockExperimentalVMOptions` so the binary's\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-387-  **tracer** writes traced metadata at runtime.\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-388-- The task **also** adds `--exact-reachability-metadata` and\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md:389:  `-H:MissingRegistrationReportingMode=Exit`. These make the **reporter** exit\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-390-  the binary with `ExitStatus.MISSING_METADATA` (172) when an access misses the\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-391-  metadata supplied via `-PmetadataConfigDirs`, instead of throwing. The gate\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-392-  routes on this exit code (§WF-native-test-verification-gate).\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-393-\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-394-Runtime behavior:\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-395-\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-396-- The task always invokes the binary the same way. There are no\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-397-  caller-supplied program arguments.\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-398-- **The tracer must record whatever the reporter reports missing.** When the\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md:399:  missing-registration reporter prints a missing dynamic-access entry to\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-400-  stdout/stderr, the same `runNativeTraceImage` invocation must add the\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-401-  equivalent entry to the trace directory passed by `-PtraceMetadataPath`. The\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-402-  metadata kind, condition package, and target identity reported to the user\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-403-  must match the entry later consumed by `mergeNativeTraceMetadata`; otherwise\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-404-  the trace loop has not produced a trustworthy runtime-observed signal. A\\n./forge/docs/workflows/native-metadata-tracing.md-405-  reporter/tracer divergence here is a Native Image defect and is handled per\\n--\\n./COVERAGE.md-1050-| `org.aspectj:aspectjrt` | [1.9.6<br>- 1.9.25](metadata/org.aspectj/aspectjrt/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> AspectJ is an aspect-oriented extension to Java, and aspectjrt provides the runtime APIs, annotations, join point types, and reflection support needed by woven AspectJ code. Applications use it at runtime to execute aspects compiled or woven with AspectJ without bundling the compiler or weaver.</details> | [100.0% (72/72 calls)](stats/org.aspectj/aspectjrt/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1051-| `org.aspectj:aspectjweaver` | [1.9.7<br>- 1.9.20](metadata/org.aspectj/aspectjweaver/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> AspectJ Weaver applies AspectJ aspects to Java classes at compile time, post-compile time, or load time. The artifact can run as a Java agent for load-time weaving and includes the runtime classes needed by woven AspectJ code.</details> | [99.4% (155/156 calls)](stats/org.aspectj/aspectjweaver/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1052-| `org.asynchttpclient:async-http-client` | [2.12.3<br>- 3.0.11](metadata/org.asynchttpclient/async-http-client/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> Async Http Client is a Java library for executing HTTP requests asynchronously and processing HTTP responses without blocking caller threads. It provides a Netty-based client API with support for common HTTP features such as WebSocket, redirects, authentication, proxies, and reactive streams integration.</details> | [100.0% (13/13 calls)](stats/org.asynchttpclient/async-http-client/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1053-| `org.asynchttpclient:async-http-client-netty-utils` | [2.12.3<br>- 2.16.0](metadata/org.asynchttpclient/async-http-client-netty-utils/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> Provides Netty utility classes for AsyncHttpClient, including helpers for converting Netty ByteBuf instances to byte arrays and strings. It supports AsyncHttpClient's nonblocking HTTP implementation by centralizing low-level Netty buffer handling used by the client modules.</details> | [100.0% (0/0 calls)](stats/org.asynchttpclient/async-http-client-netty-utils/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1054-| `org.atteo.classindex:classindex` | [3.13](metadata/org.atteo.classindex/classindex/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> ClassIndex is a Java annotation processor and runtime API that builds indexes of annotated classes, subclasses, implementors, and package classes at compile time. Applications can query those generated indexes at runtime to avoid slower classpath scanning while remaining compatible with standard Java mechanisms such as ServiceLoader.</details> | [100.0% (5/5 calls)](stats/org.atteo.classindex/classindex/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1055-| `org.attoparser:attoparser` | [2.0.5.RELEASE<br>- 2.0.9.RELEASE](metadata/org.attoparser/attoparser/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> attoparser is a Java library for parsing HTML and XML markup quickly and with low overhead. It provides event-based, DOM, selection, and output-oriented APIs for reading, transforming, and writing markup.</details> | [100.0% (4/4 calls)](stats/org.attoparser/attoparser/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md:1056:| `org.awaitility:awaitility` | [4.2.0<br>- 4.3.0](metadata/org.awaitility/awaitility/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> Awaitility is a Java DSL for waiting on asynchronous operations in tests without manual sleeps or polling loops. It lets assertions poll until conditions are satisfied or time out, making concurrent and eventually consistent behavior easier to verify.</details> | [100.0% (19/19 calls)](stats/org.awaitility/awaitility/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1057-| `org.bitbucket.b_c:jose4j` | [0.9.6](metadata/org.bitbucket.b_c/jose4j/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> jose4j is a Java library that implements the JOSE specification suite, including JSON Web Tokens, JSON Web Signature, JSON Web Encryption, and JSON Web Keys. It provides APIs for creating, parsing, signing, encrypting, decrypting, and validating JOSE objects using Java Cryptography Architecture providers.</details> | [100.0% (3/3 calls)](stats/org.bitbucket.b_c/jose4j/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1058-| `org.bouncycastle:bc-fips` | [2.1.2<br>- 2.1.3](metadata/org.bouncycastle/bc-fips/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> BC-FIPS is Bouncy Castle's FIPS 140-3 certified Java cryptographic provider and low-level API, implementing approved algorithms for use through JCE and direct APIs. Version 2.1.2 patches the 2.1 series and adds optional Intel native acceleration for AES-NI and SHA-256 on supported platforms.</details> | [24.7% (18/73 calls)](stats/org.bouncycastle/bc-fips/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1059-| `org.bouncycastle:bcpkix-jdk15on` | [1.70](metadata/org.bouncycastle/bcpkix-jdk15on/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> Bouncy Castle PKIX provides Java APIs for CMS, PKCS, EAC, TSP, CMP, CRMF, OCSP, and certificate generation. It supports parsing, creating, and processing PKIX- and certificate-related cryptographic structures on JDK 1.5 and later.</details> | [0.0% (0/6 calls)](stats/org.bouncycastle/bcpkix-jdk15on/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1060-| `org.bouncycastle:bcpkix-jdk15to18` | [1.77<br>- 1.85](metadata/org.bouncycastle/bcpkix-jdk15to18/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> Provides Bouncy Castle Java APIs for PKIX, CMS, PKCS, EAC, TSP, CMP, CRMF, OCSP, and certificate generation. It targets JDK 1.5 through JDK 1.8 and is typically used alongside a JCE/JCA provider such as Bouncy Castle's cryptography provider.</details> | [0.0% (0/12 calls)](stats/org.bouncycastle/bcpkix-jdk15to18/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1061-| `org.bouncycastle:bcpkix-jdk18on` | [1.77<br>- 1.85](metadata/org.bouncycastle/bcpkix-jdk18on/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> Bouncy Castle PKIX provides Java APIs for CMS, PKCS, EAC, TSP, CMP, CRMF, OCSP, and X.509 certificate generation on JDK 1.8 and later. It is typically used with a JCE/JCA provider such as Bouncy Castle to build and process public-key infrastructure and related cryptographic message formats.</details> | [0.0% (0/8 calls)](stats/org.bouncycastle/bcpkix-jdk18on/) |\\n./COVERAGE.md-1062-| `org.brotli:dec` | [0.1.2](metadata/org.brotli/dec/index.json) | <details><summary>Show</summary> Brotli is a lossless compression algorithm designed for compact web content and general-purpose data compression. This artifact provides the Java decoder implementation, including InputStream-based APIs for decompressing Brotli-compressed data.</details> | [100.0% (0/0 calls)](stats/org.brotli/dec/) |\\n--\\n./forge/docs/e2e.md-335--